    "durationMs": 266148,
    "finalText": "The URL shortener API is complete and fully verified. Here's what I built:\n\n**Implementation** (Rails 8.0.2 API-only, PostgreSQL via `DATABASE_URL`):\n- `app/models/link.rb` — validates url is an absolute http/https URL with a host (`URI.parse` + `URI::HTTP` + host presence), validates codes against `/\\A[A-Za-z0-9_-]{4,20}\\z/`, auto-generates unique 8-char codes when omitted, with a unique DB index on `code`\n- `app/controllers/links_controller.rb` — `POST /links` (201 with `{code, url, clicks: 0}`, 409 for duplicate codes, 422 for validation failures), `GET /links/{code}` (200/404), `GET /links` (ascending list with `items` + `total`)\n- `app/controllers/redirects_controller.rb` — `GET /r/{code}` returns 302 with the stored URL in `Location` and increments clicks via atomic `UPDATE ... clicks + 1` (exactly once per request)\n- `app/controllers/application_controller.rb` — malformed JSON returns 422. Notably, Rails 8.0.2 changed `ParseError` to inherit from `StandardError` (no longer `ActionController::BadRequest`), which initially produced a 400 — I caught it by reading the installed actionpack source and rescuing `ActionDispatch::Http::Parameters::ParseError` directly\n- `start.sh` — `rails db:prepare` (creates/migrates) then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`; dependencies install happened during the build, not at startup\n- `config/environments/production.rb` — disabled `force_ssl`/`assume_ssl` (Rails 8 defaults would have redirected plain-HTTP test traffic); README documents startup and the full API\n\n**Verification**: a 57-check test suite passed — every status code contract, URL/code validation edge cases (`https://`, `ftp://`, relative, empty, too-short/too-long/invalid codes), unchanged URL echo, exact click increments, and list ordering. I also killed and restarted the server: links and click counts persisted (`clicks=2, total=3` after restart). The test server is stopped, so port 8000 is free."
  }
